WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.540
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Friday, November 27.

2
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:19.106
Three things today: Kim Jong Un orders more tactical nukes, the Bank of Korea raises rates, and Iran warns Seoul over its Gulf deployment.

3
00:00:19.106 --> 00:00:30.960
On the first story: Kim just declared North Korea's nuclear status an irreversible reality, and he said it the same week he ignored a public overture from President Trump.

4
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:39.866
First, Kim Jong Un has inspected nuclear production sites and ordered an expansion of North Korea's tactical nuclear weapons.

5
00:00:39.866 --> 00:00:50.161
The inspections came right after Trump made a public overture toward Pyongyang at the APEC summit. North Korea's state media has not acknowledged that outreach at all.

6
00:00:50.161 --> 00:00:59.099
Kim declared his country's nuclear weapons status an, quote, "irreversible reality," and said North Korea is not seeking recognition from anyone.

7
00:00:59.099 --> 00:01:12.230
KCNA also quoted him saying, "The nuclear forces of North Korea have been built up as a powerful treasured sword to protect the sovereignty and dignity of the state and people, and their status will never change."

8
00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.237
For anyone watching the Korean peninsula, the sequencing matters.

9
00:01:16.237 --> 00:01:29.367
Trump reached out publicly; Pyongyang responded not with diplomacy but with a nuclear inspection and an order to broaden tactical nuclear deployment across multiple domains. That's not the only pressure indicator.

10
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:43.299
South Korean data submitted to a National Assembly member shows North Korean troops have crossed the Military Demarcation Line roughly 40 times over the past two months, about 30 crossings in October and around 10 in November.

11
00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:50.142
Those crossings have continued since a DMZ mine blast on September 21 that injured three South Korean soldiers.

12
00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:59.820
What to watch: whether Pyongyang makes any further public response to Trump, and whether the pace of military demarcation line crossings changes in December.

13
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:08.718
Second, the Bank of Korea has raised its benchmark policy rate to 3.25 percent, reversing a prior easing cycle.

14
00:02:08.718 --> 00:02:19.052
The hike comes against a backdrop of strong economic growth that the Wall Street Journal has attributed to the AI boom. Markets responded positively.

15
00:02:19.052 --> 00:02:32.230
Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed up about 4.3 percent on November 27, finishing at 4,915.68. The rate decision matters for policymakers tracking South Korea's economic trajectory.

16
00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:43.951
A central bank that is tightening, not easing, signals confidence in growth, but it also raises borrowing costs at a moment when households and businesses are adjusting.

17
00:02:43.951 --> 00:02:57.614
The won strengthened slightly, trading at about 1,335 to the dollar. The bottom line on the economy: a rate hike and a surging stock index on the same day paint a picture of an economy running hot.

18
00:02:57.614 --> 00:03:03.370
The question going forward is whether that strength holds if global demand softens.

19
00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:10.930
Third, Iran's Foreign Minister Abbas Araqchi has warned South Korea over its naval presence near the Gulf.

20
00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:22.930
Araqchi called South Korean Foreign Minister Cho Hyun on November 26 and told Cho that a foreign military presence in the Gulf would heighten tensions rather than contribute to peace. Cho pushed back.

21
00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:28.510
He reaffirmed that Seoul does not intend to join any war or deploy troops to the Gulf itself.

22
00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:41.350
The call came as South Korea's National Assembly reviews a government motion to extend the operating area of the Cheonghae anti-piracy unit to waters south of the Gulf of Oman. The stakes for Seoul are significant.

23
00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:54.430
South Korea sourced 70 percent of its oil from the Middle East in 2025, with most of those shipments moving through the Strait of Hormuz. That energy dependence gives Araqchi's warning real weight in this conversation.

24
00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.950
The National Assembly's review is ongoing.

25
00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:07.750
Watch whether the opposition uses the warning to challenge the government's motion, and whether Cho's reassurances are enough to keep the channel with Iran's Foreign Ministry open.

26
00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:12.024
Now to Pyongyang. KCNA led with two stories today.

27
00:04:12.024 --> 00:04:26.288
The first, Kim's inspection of nuclear facilities and his order for broader tactical nuclear deployment, was the brief's lead story; state media framed it as Kim reaffirming the permanence and power of North Korea's nuclear arsenal.

28
00:04:26.288 --> 00:04:39.998
The second article reported that Kim sent a letter to a conference of Workers' Party cell leaders, stressing discipline and the faithful relay of party directives, a signal of ideological tightening at the grassroots level.

29
00:04:39.998 --> 00:04:49.343
Kim also attended a performance by a Russian art troupe in Pyongyang, which KCNA covered as a demonstration of expanding North Korea-Russia cooperation.

30
00:04:49.343 --> 00:04:59.610
The bottom line from Pyongyang: state media pushed nuclear strength and Russian friendship simultaneously, while maintaining complete silence on Trump's APEC outreach.

31
00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:11.175
The rest of the news, quickly. Ukrainian intelligence reported that North Korea has temporarily halted missile shipments to Russia. No explanation for the pause was given.

32
00:05:11.175 --> 00:05:23.541
Russia and North Korea are discussing direct flights between Moscow and Wonsan. That conversation is happening inside the Russia-North Korea intergovernmental commission on trade and economic cooperation.

33
00:05:23.541 --> 00:05:34.573
North Korea and Russia also signed a classified information protection agreement in March of this year, N.K. News reported. The agreement covers Russia's highest-level state secrets.

34
00:05:34.573 --> 00:05:43.787
A North Korean defector was detained Thursday evening moving north toward the inter-Korean border fence in Cheorwon, north of the Civilian Control Line.

35
00:05:43.787 --> 00:05:55.183
Six North Korean fishermen rescued while drifting in the Sea of Japan in early November were repatriated Friday morning through the Northern Limit Line. One chose to remain in South Korea.

36
00:05:55.183 --> 00:06:06.942
The Unification Ministry confirmed the repatriation. North Korean hackers are laundering cryptocurrency stolen from Binance through obscure online services, Reuters reported, citing researchers.

37
00:06:06.942 --> 00:06:20.278
The Chinese social media platform Weibo has blocked searches for the South Korean term for North Korea in Chinese characters, N.K. News found, in a pattern consistent with filtering content North Korea finds undesirable.

38
00:06:20.278 --> 00:06:30.522
China has also reversed a previously discussed plan to loan a pair of pandas to Seoul. Beijing cited South Korean comments on Taiwan as the reason for the abrupt change.

39
00:06:30.522 --> 00:06:39.978
China, Japan, South Korea and ASEAN member states have launched formal talks to form a working group on cross-border digital payments and crypto regulation.

40
00:06:39.978 --> 00:06:49.858
In domestic politics, the ruling Democratic Party's ethics committee suspended Supreme Council member Representative Choi Min-hee's party membership for two years.

41
00:06:49.858 --> 00:06:57.314
The penalty stems from remarks suggesting President Lee Jae-myung had opposed former President Yoon Suk Yeol's impeachment.

42
00:06:57.314 --> 00:07:03.800
If finalized, Choi would be ineligible to run on the Democratic Party ticket in the 2028 general elections.

43
00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:16.884
The National Assembly's Legislation and Judiciary Committee adopted the confirmation hearing report for Justice Minister nominee Lee Yong-chul, with People Power Party members declining to participate.

44
00:07:16.884 --> 00:07:23.779
If confirmed, Lee would be the first justice minister appointed after the abolition of the prosecution service.

45
00:07:23.779 --> 00:07:31.232
Unification Minister nominee Hong Ik-pyo disclosed assets of approximately 6.49 billion won at his confirmation hearing.

46
00:07:31.232 --> 00:07:43.095
South Korea's second-largest hypermarket chain, Homeplus, has shut all its stores temporarily, saying it cannot sustain operating losses. Shoppers and tenant vendors were left without notice.

47
00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:55.269
A Gallup Korea poll released today found that 57 percent of respondents describe the December 3 martial law declaration as an insurrection. That figure is down 14 percentage points over two years.

48
00:07:55.269 --> 00:08:06.697
Gallup's separate approval tracking, taken November 3 to 5, shows presidential approval at 41 percent, with the Democratic Party at 36 percent and the People Power Party at 28 percent.

49
00:08:06.697 --> 00:08:22.039
The International Institute for Strategic Studies has published an analysis arguing that South Korea's defense cooperation with Europe has matured beyond arms sales into a strategic partnership with long-term implications for both sides' security.

50
00:08:22.039 --> 00:08:25.952
That report maps the relationship since the Warsaw NATO summit.

51
00:08:25.952 --> 00:08:35.952
Looking ahead: a pan-government task force on a unified military academy launches December 2, drawing personnel from the military, education and land ministries.

52
00:08:35.952 --> 00:08:41.790
South Korea's Defense Ministry says it plans to expand reserve drone companies to 100 by 2030.

53
00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:54.906
Beyond the region. Korea Hydro and Nuclear Power is accelerating its push to finalize the Czech nuclear power plant project, with Seoul working to expedite selection of a new contractor.

54
00:08:54.906 --> 00:09:08.680
India and South Korea announced 25 specific outcomes from their bilateral summit and elevated their relationship to what they called a "futuristic partnership," covering defense, technology and economic cooperation.

55
00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:25.682
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed up about 4.3 percent on November 27, finishing at 4,915.68. Brent crude fell about 2.7 percent to around 92 dollars a barrel.

56
00:09:25.682 --> 00:09:38.340
The dollar bought about 1,335 won. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Friday, November 27. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
